    "justification": "On the strict-interior subclass, the reduction checks that the corrected envelope, including its composition term, collapses to the established two-group panel-DiD bound.",
    "gap": "SantAnnaZhao2020 gives the panel influence function in Proposition 1(a), equation (2.11), and its bound in equation (2.12), but not as a PT-or-SC union endpoint.",
    "consumer": "The reduction is a theoretical strict-interior benchmark for the two-period panel-DiD case of the integrated analysis; it does not introduce a second procedure.",
    "proof_tex": "At the stated strict-interior constant-rank point, \\(\\mathrm{lem{:}tangent\\text{-}normal\\text{-}form}\\) and \\(\\mathrm{thm{:}union\\text{-}envelope}\\) apply. If \\(J=1\\), then \\(\\mathbf 1_J\\mathbf 1_J^{\\prime}=1\\), and \\(\\mathrm{def{:}restriction\\text{-}maps}\\) gives\n\\[\n A_{\\mathrm{PT}}=I_1-1=0,\n \\qquad r_{\\mathrm{PT},x}=0.\n \\tag{1}\n\\]\nThe tangent-normal form therefore gives\n\\[\n \\mathcal T_{\\mathrm{PT}}(P_0)=\\mathcal H_{P_0},\n \\qquad \\mathcal I_{\\mathrm{PT}}(P_0)=\\{\\phi_{\\mathrm{PT}}\\},\n \\tag{2}\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\phi_{\\mathrm{PT}}=\\chi_{\\mathrm{PT}}+\\sum_xg^{\\circ}_{\\mathrm{PT},x}\\); in particular, the treated-composition term has not been dropped.\n\nWith one donor, the intercept row of the SC restriction forces \\(w_2(x)=1\\), while its preperiod equations give \\(\\mu_{1,t}(x)=\\mu_{2,t}(x)\\) for \\(t<T\\). Hence throughout the SC branch,\n\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n \\theta_{\\mathrm{PT}}(P_O)\n &=\\sum_x\\nu_x\\{\\mu_{1,T}-\\mu_{1,T-1}-\\mu_{2,T}+\\mu_{2,T-1}\\}\\\\\n &=\\sum_x\\nu_x\\{\\mu_{1,T}-\\mu_{2,T}\\}\n =\\theta_{\\mathrm{SC}}(P_O).\n \\end{aligned}\n \\tag{3}\n\\]\nTheir derivatives agree on every SC tangent. Because the singleton PT influence function represents the PT derivative on all of \\(\\mathcal H_{P_0}\\), it represents the common derivative on every SC tangent and therefore belongs to \\(\\mathcal I_{\\mathrm{SC}}(P_0)\\). Thus\n\\[\n \\mathcal I_{\\mathrm{PT}}(P_0)\\subseteq\\mathcal I_{\\mathrm{SC}}(P_0),\n \\qquad \\phi_U=\\phi_{\\mathrm{PT}},\n \\qquad V_U=V_{\\mathrm{PT}}.\n \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(T=2\\), write \\(\\Delta Y=Y_2-Y_1\\) and \\(m_g(x)=E_O[\\Delta Y\\mid G=g,X=x]\\). The strict overlap conditions make every denominator positive. Direct quotient and conditional-mean differentiation yields\n\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n \\phi_{\\mathrm{PT}}(O)\n &=\\frac{\\mathbf 1\\{G=1\\}}{\\pi_1}\\{\\Delta Y-m_1(X)\\}\n -\\frac{\\mathbf 1\\{G=2\\}}{\\pi_1}\\frac{p_1(X)}{p_2(X)}\\{\\Delta Y-m_2(X)\\}\\\\\n &\\quad+\\frac{\\mathbf 1\\{G=1\\}}{\\pi_1}\\{m_1(X)-m_2(X)-\\theta\\}.\n \\end{aligned}\n \\tag{5}\n\\]\nThe first two terms are conditionally centered outcome-residual terms and the last is the treated-composition gradient. Their sum is mean zero, and its covariance with every score equals the derivative of the panel ATT functional. Under the group relabeling, (5) is the panel efficient influence function in SantAnnaZhao2020, Proposition 1(a), equation (2.11); equation (2.12) is its variance bound, exactly as recorded by \\(\\mathrm{lem{:}santanna\\text{-}zhao\\text{-}panel\\text{-}did\\text{-}gradient}\\).",
